::ALG2_U06_L06_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q01
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A fundraiser has $200 and earns $15 per item. Which expression models total money after x items?
{
=A. 200+15x#Correct. Add starting money and earnings per item.
~B. 200x+15#This treats the starting money as the per-item rate.
~C. 15x-200#This subtracts the starting amount incorrectly.
~D. 200/15x#This does not represent repeated item earnings.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q02
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which inequality represents needing at least 1000 dollars?
{
~A. M < 1000#This means less than 1000, not at least.
=B. M >= 1000#Correct. At least means greater than or equal to.
~C. M = 1000 only#At least includes 1000 and values greater than 1000.
~D. M <= 1000#This means no more than 1000.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q03
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Solve 250+20x >= 850.
{
~A. x >= 20#This subtracts incorrectly or divides before isolating.
~B. x >= 25#This does not account for the full difference from 250 to 850.
=C. x >= 30#Correct. 20x >= 600, so x >= 30.
~D. x >= 55#This adds 250 and 850 before dividing.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q04
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A calculation gives 12.4 buses needed. How many buses are needed?
{
~A. 12#Rounding down leaves some people without a bus.
~B. 12.4#A fractional bus is not realistic.
~C. 13.4#The count should be a whole number.
=D. 13#Correct. Bus counts must be whole numbers, and the constraint requires rounding up.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q05
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which is an assumption in a simple savings model?
{
=A. The rate stays the same over the time period.#Correct. Many simple models assume a constant rate.
~B. The answer has no units.#Units are not an assumption; they are required for interpretation.
~C. The model cannot be checked.#Models should be checked against context.
~D. The variables are meaningless.#Variables should have clear meanings.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q06
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is a constraint?
{
~A. A random guess#A constraint is based on context, not guessing.
=B. A limit or requirement from the context#Correct. Constraints restrict possible solutions.
~C. The color of the graph#Graph color is not a mathematical constraint.
~D. A deleted variable#Variables are defined and used, not deleted.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q07
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Why should units be included in a final answer?
{
~A. Units make every answer larger.#Units do not change numerical size.
~B. Units replace calculations.#Calculations are still needed.
=C. Units explain what the number represents.#Correct. Units connect the result to the context.
~D. Units are optional in modeling.#Units are essential for interpretation.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q08
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A model predicts negative time. What should you do?
{
~A. Accept it because equations are always right.#A mathematical answer must still make sense in context.
~B. Change time to money.#Changing units does not fix the invalid result.
~C. Ignore the negative sign.#The sign is part of the solution and must be interpreted.
=D. Reject or revise it because time cannot be negative in the context.#Correct. Context makes negative time invalid.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q09
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which is the best first step in modeling?
{
=A. Understand the context and define variables.#Correct. Variables and context guide the model.
~B. Solve before reading the question.#Solving requires a model and defined variables.
~C. Round all numbers immediately.#Rounding too early can distort the model.
~D. Ignore constraints until after submission.#Constraints must be considered before finalizing.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q10
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A model P=500(1.08)ᵗ predicts population. What does 1.08 mean?
{
~A. 108 people are added each year.#The factor represents percent growth, not a fixed count.
=B. The population grows by 8% each time period.#Correct. A factor of 1.08 means 8% growth.
~C. The initial population is 1.08.#The initial population is 500.
~D. The time period is 1.08 years.#The exponent t represents time periods.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q11
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A habitat can support at most 1,000 animals. A model predicts 1,150. What is the issue?
{
~A. The model has no output.#The model does have an output.
~B. The prediction is below capacity.#1150 is above 1000, so the prediction is not below the habitat capacity.
=C. The prediction violates the maximum constraint.#Correct. The model output is greater than the habitat limit.
~D. The input must be negative.#The issue is the output constraint, not input sign.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q12
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What is model validation?
{
~A. Making the answer longer#Length does not validate a model.
~B. Removing all assumptions#Assumptions should be checked, not simply removed.
~C. Choosing the first equation available#Validation requires evidence.
=D. Checking whether the model works for the context and data#Correct. Validation tests reasonableness and fit.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q13
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
A taxi charges $4 plus $2.50 per mile. Which model gives cost C for m miles?
{
=A. C=4+2.50m#Correct. The fixed fee is added to the per-mile charge.
~B. C=4m+2.50#This swaps the fixed fee and per-mile rate.
~C. C=2.50/m+4#This rational expression does not model repeated miles.
~D. C=4(2.50)ᵐ#This is exponential and does not match a per-mile fee.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q14
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Using C=4+2.50m, what is the cost for 8 miles?
{
~A. $20#This uses only the per-mile charge.
=B. $24#Correct. C=4+2.50(8)=24.
~C. $32#This multiplies the fixed fee by miles.
~D. $10.50#This adds 4, 2.50, and 8 without applying the model.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q15
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which answer best interprets x >= 30 in a ticket-selling model?
{
~A. Sell exactly 30 tickets only.#Greater values also satisfy the inequality.
~B. Sell fewer than 30 tickets.#That would not meet x >= 30.
=C. Sell at least 30 tickets.#Correct. The inequality includes 30 and all larger ticket counts.
~D. Sell 30 dollars of tickets.#x represents tickets, not dollars.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q16
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which is a good revision after a model overpredicts later data?
{
~A. Ignore the later data.#Data should be used to evaluate the model.
~B. Use the same prediction without comment.#A known overprediction needs explanation or revision.
~C. Delete the context.#Context is needed to revise the model.
=D. Try a different model type or limit the prediction range.#Correct. Overprediction suggests the model may not apply as far out.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q17
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which is most important when communicating a model result?
{
=A. State the result with units and context.#Correct. A model result must be interpreted in the situation.
~B. Give only the equation number.#An equation alone does not communicate the result.
~C. Remove all variables.#Variables help explain the model.
~D. Use no words after solving.#Interpretation is part of modeling.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q18
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which model type is reasonable for repeated percent growth?
{
~A. linear#Linear models use repeated addition.
=B. exponential#Correct. Repeated percent growth uses repeated multiplication.
~C. absolute value#Absolute value models have V-shaped behavior.
~D. reciprocal#Reciprocal models involve inverse variation, not percent growth.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q19
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
Which model type is reasonable for a fixed cost plus a per-item cost?
{
~A. exponential#Exponential models use percent or ratio change.
~B. quadratic#Quadratic models involve squared input behavior.
=C. linear#Correct. Fixed cost plus constant per-item cost creates a linear model.
~D. logarithmic#Logarithmic models do not represent fixed plus per-item cost.
}


::ALG2_U06_L06_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U06_L06_Q20
MLA Standard: Support-only synthesis; no new primary MLA standard
Question:
What does it mean to revise a model?
{
~A. Ignore all previous work.#Revision uses previous work as evidence.
~B. Change the answer randomly.#Revision should be based on context or data.
~C. Make the equation longer.#Longer equations are not automatically better.
=D. Adjust the model based on evidence or constraints.#Correct. Revision improves the model using what was learned.
}

